          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "**Not always.** The reference material I searched supports part of your question, but it does not cover payer rules.\n\n**What the sources say:**\n- An ICD-10-CM code is three to seven characters. The three-character form is the category, and characters four to six add detail such as cause, site, severity, or laterality. A seventh character, where a category uses one, describes the encounter, such as initial or subsequent treatment. (ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions)\n- Most categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before the code is billable. Some are complete at three characters. Hypertension code I10 is one, so a request to add specificity to it cannot be met. (Coding hypertension: I10)\n- The same logic applies to other categories. Type 2 diabetes is E11 and has subcategories for complications, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is J44 with its own fourth-character subdivisions. (Coding type 2 diabetes; Coding COPD: J44)\n\n**What the sources do not cover:** They do not say how payers apply their edits, whether a payer may insist on the most specific code, or how to tell when a payer has misapplied an edit. I also could not find anything on whether the record already supports the added detail. I have not filled those gaps from memory.\n\n**Practical next steps:**\n1. Ask the payer which specific code they want and which rule or edit triggered the request.\n2. Have the claim reviewed by a certified medical coder or your billing office. They can check the code against the official code set and the payer's policy.\n3. Compare the clinical note with the requested detail. If the note does not document it, the request may be fair. If it does, the request may be an error.\n\nIf you share the code and the payer's exact wording, I can look for more specific guidance on that code.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
